### Description

csr and csc to array is really slow and it would nice to speed it up.

### Additional Information

```
from cuml.common.kernel_utils import cuda_kernel_factory
import math
from typing import Literal

import cupy as cp
from cupyx.scipy.sparse import issparse, isspmatrix_csc, isspmatrix_csr, spmatrix

_sparse2dense = r"""
(const int* indptr,const int *index,const {0} *data,
            {0}* out, int major, int minor, int c_switch) {
        int row = blockIdx.x*blockDim.x+threadIdx.x ;
        int col = blockIdx.y*blockDim.y+threadIdx.y ;
        if(row >= major){
            return;
        }
        int start = indptr[row];
        int stop = indptr[row+1];
        if (col>= (stop - start)){
            return;
        }
        int idx = index[start + col];
        long long int res_index ;
        if (c_switch== 1){
            res_index = static_cast<long long int>(row)*minor+idx;
        }
        else{
            res_index = static_cast<long long int>(row)+idx*major;
        }
        atomicAdd(&out[res_index], data[start + col]);
}
"""


def _sparse2densekernel(dtype):
    return cuda_kernel_factory(_sparse2dense, (dtype,), "_sparse2dense")



def _sparse_to_dense(X: spmatrix, order: Literal["C", "F"] | None = None) -> cp.ndarray:
    if order is None:
        order = "C"

    if isspmatrix_csr(X):
        major, minor = X.shape[0], X.shape[1]
        switcher = 1 if order == "C" else 0
    elif isspmatrix_csc(X):
        major, minor = X.shape[1], X.shape[0]
        switcher = 0 if order == "C" else 1
    else:
        raise ValueError("Input matrix must be a sparse `csc` or `csr` matrix")
    sparse2dense = _sparse2densekernel(X.dtype)

    dense = cp.zeros(X.shape, order=order, dtype=X.dtype)
    max_nnz = cp.diff(X.indptr).max()
    tpb = (32, 32)
    bpg_x = math.ceil(major / tpb[0])
    bpg_y = math.ceil(max_nnz / tpb[1])
    bpg = (bpg_x, bpg_y)

    sparse2dense(
        bpg,
        tpb,
        (X.indptr, X.indices, X.data, dense, major, minor, switcher),
    )
    return dense
```
I wrote this code for [rapids-singlecell](https://github.com/scverse/rapids_singlecell). And in my benchmarks its as fast as the cusparse primatives while also  enabling C-Arrays.

### Description

### Preamble

There is a common pattern for functions in `scipy.special` where something like this happens: an array or matrix of coefficients is generated based on some of the functions arguments, and an expansion is then computed using these coefficients together with some of the other arguments. The scalar kernels of such functions in SciPy,  tend to allocate large arrays internally to store the coefficients, leading to redundant work in some cases, and making these kernels wholly unfit for use in CuPy.

In SciPy, I am proposing to manage such cases by decomposing such functions into gufuncs: a gufunc to generate the coefficients, and another that performs a reduction operation over it. There are other cases to, but this is the general idea. It's not always coefficients, but the story is similar.

The PR, https://github.com/scipy/scipy/pull/24894, gives an example of this pattern for the simplest case I could find.

<details>

At the Python level these first have gufuncs to generate the pmf or cdf of the Poisson Binomial distribution, which is done with an inherently serial recurrence relation, and then additional ones to select appropriate values from these distributions.

```python
def _poisson_binom_pmf(k, p):
    xp = array_namespace(k, p)
    n = p.shape[-1]
    out_shape = p.shape[:-1] + (n + 1,)
    pmf = xp.zeros(out_shape, dtype=p.dtype)
    _poisson_binom_pmf_all(p, out=pmf)
    return _take_from_pmf(pmf, k)


def _poisson_binom_cdf(k, p):
    xp = array_namespace(k, p)
    n = p.shape[-1]
    out_shape = p.shape[:-1] + (n + 1,)
    cdf = xp.zeros(out_shape, dtype=p.dtype)
    _poisson_binom_cdf_all(p, out=cdf)
    return _take_from_discrete_cdf(cdf, k)
```

The kernels in `xsf` for the core computation are very simple

```C++
template <typename InputMat, typename OutputMat>
XSF_HOST_DEVICE inline void poisson_binom_pmf_all(InputMat p, OutputMat res) {
    /* Outputs the full pmf of a Poisson-Binomial distribution
     *
     * p should be an std::mdspan view of a 1d array of length n containing
     * the success probabilities for the n Bernoulli trials. res should be
     * a std::mdspan view of a 1d array of zeros of length n + 1. It is up to
     * the caller to pass valid values for p and res.
     *
     * Upon completion, res(k) will contain the probability of observing k
     * successes for k from 0 to n.
     */
    using T = typename OutputMat::value_type;
    auto n = p.extent(0);
    auto out_size = res.extent(0);

    if (out_size != n + 1) {
        set_error("poisson_binom_pmf", SF_ERROR_MEMORY, "out.shape[-1] must be p.shape[-1] + 1");
    }

    if (n == 0) {
        res(0) = T(1.0);
        return;
    }

    res(0) = T(1.0) - p(0);
    res(1) = p(0);

    for (decltype(n) i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        T p_i = p(i);
        T q_i = 1 - p_i;
        for (decltype(n) j = i + 1; j >= 1; j--) {
            T tmp = res(j - 1) * p_i;
            res(j - 1) *= q_i;
            res(j) += tmp;
        }
    }
}

template <typename InputMat, typename OutputMat>
XSF_HOST_DEVICE inline void poisson_binom_cdf_all(InputMat p, OutputMat res) {
    auto n = res.extent(0);
    poisson_binom_pmf_all(p, res);
    for (decltype(n) i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        res(i) += res(i - 1);
    }
}

template <typename InputMat>
XSF_HOST_DEVICE inline typename InputMat::value_type take_from_pmf(InputMat pmf, long long int k) {
    using T = typename InputMat::value_type;
    auto size = pmf.extent(0);
    if ((k < 0) || (k >= static_cast<long long int>(size))) {
        return T(0.0);
    }
    return pmf(k);
}

template <typename InputMat>
XSF_HOST_DEVICE inline typename InputMat::value_type take_from_discrete_cdf(InputMat cdf, long long int k) {
    using T = typename InputMat::value_type;
    auto size = cdf.extent(0);
    if (k < 0) {
        return T(0.0);
    }
    if (k >= static_cast<long long int>(size) - 1) {
        return T(1.0);
    }
    return cdf(k);
}
```

where template parameters `InputMat` and `OutputMat` are meant to be instantiated as `std::mdspan` views.

</details>

### Request
I had originally thought that CuPy's [GeneralizedUFunc](https://docs.cupy.dev/en/stable/reference/generated/cupyx.GeneralizedUFunc.html) class can accomplish what I want. It handles things like signature parsing, but looking into it, it is currently  for creating something that behaves like a gufunc by looping at the Python level over an inherently parallel core computation. In the issue that lead to the creation of CuPy's `GeneralizedUFunc` it appears that @seberg points out this distinction here (https://github.com/cupy/cupy/issues/2552#issuecomment-601246181)

*The idea of gufuncs is that dispatching occurs on a 1-D vectors of the computational subarrays. I feel for you it may depend on the gufunc whether dispatching to the GPU should occur for each subarray or for a vector of subarrays.*

I would like for there to be something like `ElementwiseKernel`, but where the core computation is not necessarily scalar, so that it's straightforward to take kernels of the kind I showed for `poisson_binom` above and create the kind of gufuncs I want.

### Additional Information

I would be willing to work on contributing this feature since it would be an important part of my plans for `xsf`. I would like to do it in such a way that makes minimal changes to the existing infra in CuPy, and can devote some time into understanding the codebase to figure out the best way to do things. @leofang I would appreciate any advice or pointers to relevant information that you might have to offer.
